1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:08,195
What if we have a billion examples?

2
00:00:08,195 --> 00:00:13,214
The full gradient sums over every one of them, which is a lot of work for a single step.

3
00:00:13,214 --> 00:00:22,133
So instead we grab a random mini-batch and average its gradients, and on average we get the full gradient back.

4
00:00:22,133 --> 00:00:28,454
Here are six per-example gradients at one point, and their average, the full gradient, is two thirds.

5
00:00:28,454 --> 00:00:30,708
Now try every possible batch of two.

6
00:00:30,708 --> 00:00:36,533
Those batch averages land all over the place, from minus two up to three and a quarter.

7
00:00:36,533 --> 00:00:41,888
Average all fifteen of them and you get exactly two thirds, which is what unbiased means.

8
00:00:41,888 --> 00:00:46,433
And yet five of the fifteen point the wrong way.

9
00:00:46,433 --> 00:00:48,069
So is the noise a bad thing?

10
00:00:48,069 --> 00:00:52,509
On a bumpy surface like this one, it might shake us out of a shallow valley.

11
00:00:52,509 --> 00:00:58,526
But the same noise can just as easily kick us somewhere worse, so it's a possibility and not a promise.

12
00:00:58,526 --> 00:01:07,466
Far from the answer a rough direction is plenty, but close to it the noise is what holds us back.

13
00:01:07,466 --> 00:01:10,428
So when does SGD converge with a constant step?

14
00:01:10,428 --> 00:01:14,145
Let's take the case where every example can be fit exactly.

15
00:01:14,145 --> 00:01:20,800
We start from zero, and each step picks one random example and follows just that example's gradient.

16
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:23,801
Now track the error from the minimum-norm solution.

17
00:01:23,801 --> 00:01:29,566
Because the fit is exact, that error follows the very same random update.

18
00:01:29,566 --> 00:01:32,530
Zoom in on one step and expand the squared error.

19
00:01:32,530 --> 00:01:37,309
This first line is exact, and it says a good step subtracts something positive.

20
00:01:37,309 --> 00:01:44,200
Swap that factor for its worst case, and as long as eta is below one over rho squared we get an upper bound.

21
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:46,676
Now average over which example we drew.

22
00:01:46,676 --> 00:01:52,708
Every example is equally likely, so the sum turns into the squared length of X times the error.

23
00:01:52,708 --> 00:01:56,708
The error lives in the row space, so that length can't be tiny.

24
00:01:56,708 --> 00:02:03,266
On average, then, each step keeps at most a fraction alpha of the squared error.

25
00:02:03,266 --> 00:02:06,319
Let's try it with two equations and three unknowns.

26
00:02:06,319 --> 00:02:09,612
With these numbers alpha comes out as zero point seven,

27
00:02:09,612 --> 00:02:14,102
so on average at most seventy percent of the squared error survives a step.

28
00:02:14,102 --> 00:02:16,377
Single runs jitter, but they all fall.

29
00:02:16,377 --> 00:02:22,966
Their average sits just under the bound, which is a straight line on this log scale.

30
00:02:22,966 --> 00:02:24,883
Why does a fixed step work here?

31
00:02:24,883 --> 00:02:30,994
At the solution every example's gradient is zero, so the noise vanishes exactly where we want to stop.

32
00:02:30,994 --> 00:02:32,612
But this is a special case.

33
00:02:32,612 --> 00:02:36,267
It needs an exact fit, full row rank and a small enough step,

34
00:02:36,267 --> 00:02:41,133
and with noisy labels a constant step stalls some distance from the answer.

35
00:02:41,133 --> 00:02:43,511
There's one last subtlety in the proof.

36
00:02:43,511 --> 00:02:49,366
Shrinking at every step isn't enough on its own, because the error could still stall above zero.

37
00:02:49,366 --> 00:02:55,233
The fixed factor alpha, strictly below one, is what rules that out.

